SCOPE ANSWER: the browser-reload half is WORSE than this issue describes, and two of the three holes SURVIVE the fix in 63e101b. Read this before scoping.

The issue frames HTTP history as the working case and the other three pages as the broken ones. That framing is wrong. The guard shape is what decides whether the browser reloads, and on EVERY page including HTTP history it is:

    if (!selected) return;        // <- returns BEFORE preventDefault
    event.preventDefault();

So Ctrl+R reaches the browser and reloads the console in three situations, only one of which this issue names:

  HOLE 1 (named, now fixed on 2 of 3 pages). A selected row on Site map / Logger / WebSockets history. Fixed on Site map and WebSockets history. Logger cannot be fixed from the UI — ToolLogRow has no message or exchange id, so there is nothing to send; see my earlier comment.

  HOLE 2 (NOT named, NOT fixed, present on HTTP history too). No row selected. Nothing is selected on a fresh page load, so the FIRST Ctrl+R an operator presses after opening the console reloads it — on the page this issue says works. Same on Site map and WebSockets history after my fix.

  HOLE 3 (NOT named, NOT fixed, present on HTTP history too). Focus in a form field. The bindings pass `enableOnFormTags: false`, so the handler does not run at all when focus is in an input, textarea or select — no handler means no preventDefault means the browser reloads. On HTTP history the DSL query box and the three column-filter inputs are the most-used controls on the page, and the operator's hands are in one of them most of the time. This is very likely the most common way the console actually gets reloaded in practice, and it is the one nobody has written down.

I PRESERVED holes 2 and 3 deliberately rather than silently changing behaviour: the pre-existing history.tsx semantics were 'only claim the key when there is something to send', and unifying five pages onto one hook was already the change under review. That was a judgement call and I think it is the wrong long-term answer — flagging it rather than acting on it.

WHAT THE FIX SHOULD BE, precisely, for whoever picks this up. On any page carrying a row table, `mod+r` should call preventDefault unconditionally and then decide what to do:

    useHotkeys(chord, (event) => {
      event.preventDefault();               // always - this page owns the key
      if (!exchange) { toast.info("Select a row to send it to Repeater"); return; }
      void actions.sendToRepeater(exchange);
    }, { enableOnFormTags: true, enableOnContentEditable: true }, [...]);

Two changes from what is there now: preventDefault moves above the guard, and `enableOnFormTags` flips to true. The toast is what makes the swallow honest instead of a dead key — without it, unconditional preventDefault turns 'reload' into 'nothing happens', which is a different bad outcome. `enableOnFormTags: true` is what intercept.tsx already uses for proxy.forward, so there is in-repo precedent.

RISK TO WEIGH, and the reason I did not just do it: `mod+r` is the browser's reload and an operator may genuinely want to reload the console. Swallowing it on every table page with no escape means the only reload left is F5 / Ctrl+Shift+R. Burp is a desktop app and never faced this. This is a product decision, not a bug fix, which is why it belongs in your inventory rather than in a patch from me.

VALIDITY: the issue is valid and the P2 is arguably too low given hole 3, but its DESCRIPTION should be corrected — HTTP history is not the working baseline it is presented as.
